  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 25,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "January 4, 1617",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 107",
    "excerpt": "Some months have already passed since my last was sent to Your Paternity — which I did so as not to trouble you, and all the more because I was certain that, should some occasion arise, Your Paternity, for your great charity and love toward your sons, would not have failed to console me, unworthy as I am — especially since it concerns a thing so important for my salvation. But the occasion of the captivity of my brother, Father Giuseppe Balami, has so enkindled my heart and inflamed it in such a manner that every day I weep, with a holy envy of him; for I would have wished it had happened to me, or at least would yet happen to me — the same — so that in this way a beginning might be made by me to satisfy, in some small part, my Lord for the multitude of my sins.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Some months have already passed since my last was sent to Your Paternity — which I did so as not to trouble you, and all the more because I was certain that, should some occasion arise, Your Paternity, for your great charity and love toward your sons, would not have failed to console me, unworthy as I am — especially since it concerns a thing so important for my salvation. But the occasion of the captivity of my brother, Father Giuseppe Balami, has so enkindled my heart and inflamed it in such a manner that every day I weep, with a holy envy of him; for I would have wished it had happened to me, or at least would yet happen to me — the same — so that in this way a beginning might be made by me to satisfy, in some small part, my Lord for the multitude of my sins. Most loving Father General, they are such and so many that, if I do not come to shed my blood, I know not how I shall begin to make satisfaction; and Your Paternity must know that what I write to you is written with my face to the ground, and not without tears — though with great hope that the same Lord, hanging on the cross for my sins, may have inspired it in me, so that, while my age assists me, I may employ myself in His service, for I am already twenty-five years old. I pray Your Paternity in visceribus Iesu Christi (by the tender mercies of Jesus Christ) to remember me. I do not ask for the East Indies nor the West Indies, except where Your Paternity, guided by the Holy Spirit, shall judge that I may suffer much [and much]; for many, indeed very many, are my sins — and I assure Your Paternity that I shall never forget, though unworthily, amid labors and sweat, to pray the Lord to rain down upon Your Paternity all His graces. Do not abandon me, do not leave me, Your Paternity — for O, how it burns, how kindled is my soul! O, how it thinks of this, how gladly it speaks of this, and will never tire of speaking of it, since so God, who governs it, gives us to understand. I finish, most loving Father General, without ever finishing, standing with my face to the ground and deeming myself unworthy of so great a grace — not doubting, however, that the Lord who calls me will give me the grace and strength to serve Him. Humbly, then, I ask Your Paternity's blessing, praying for you from heaven every contentment, and commending myself greatly to your Holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 39,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "December 31, 1617",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 273",
    "excerpt": "Desirous of seeing myself employed in the help of souls in the most extreme parts of the world, that I may in some way begin to show myself grateful for the mercy shown me by my Jesus, I come with this letter to pray Your Paternity to deign to remember me and to expose me in whatever place shall please you, as I have written at other times; and I tell you in truth, Father General, that the Lord has so penetrated my soul with this affection that a thousand years seems to me every hour; indeed I take consolation in nothing else but in thinking that Your Paternity, guided by the Holy Spirit, will soon console me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Desirous of seeing myself employed in the help of souls in the most extreme parts of the world, that I may in some way begin to show myself grateful for the mercy shown me by my Jesus, I come with this letter to pray Your Paternity to deign to remember me and to expose me in whatever place shall please you, as I have written at other times; and I tell you in truth, Father General, that the Lord has so penetrated my soul with this affection that a thousand years seems to me every hour; indeed I take consolation in nothing else but in thinking that Your Paternity, guided by the Holy Spirit, will soon console me. For when I think of my sins, the business of my salvation seems to me a very difficult thing, if I do not come to shed my blood for my Jesus, or at least if I do not expose myself to this. So, to trouble you no more, with tears in my eyes I pray Your Paternity to console me, and not to regard my weakness of spirit and ability; for I hope that the Lord who calls me and gives me this affection and desire will give me such strength of spirit that I may serve Him as I ought, to show myself grateful. Again, for the sake of the mysteries of the mercy of Jesus Christ, on my knees and weeping, I commend to you the business of my salvation; and let Your Paternity believe me, Father General, that I feel my strength failing me — consolation, consolation! I do not cease, nor shall I cease, unworthily though it be, to pray the Lord for the health of Your Paternity, that the Society may long enjoy such a Father. Humbly I ask your blessing, persuading myself that I shall soon be consoled."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 62,
    "year": 1626,
    "date": "January 15, 1626",
    "place": "Messina",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 316",
    "excerpt": "With this occasion of the coming of Father Basile, our minister of the College, I have not wished to fail in greeting Your Paternity, so that even in this way the great ardor and desire of my heart may be somewhat vented: to see myself employed, as I wrote to Your Paternity, in the harvest of souls in the farthest parts of the world, so that by this means I may do some little thing pleasing in the service of His Divine Majesty, so often and so grievously offended by me. And Your Paternity should know that no other motive is found in me for this but the desire to begin to satisfy God, by this path, for the multitude of my sins — which in no other way could so effectively succeed for me but by this act of exposing myself, putting my life at stake in the farthest parts of the world, for Him who for my love died upon a hard wood of the Cross.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "With this occasion of the coming of Father Basile, our minister of the College, I have not wished to fail in greeting Your Paternity, so that even in this way the great ardor and desire of my heart may be somewhat vented: to see myself employed, as I wrote to Your Paternity, in the harvest of souls in the farthest parts of the world, so that by this means I may do some little thing pleasing in the service of His Divine Majesty, so often and so grievously offended by me. And Your Paternity should know that no other motive is found in me for this but the desire to begin to satisfy God, by this path, for the multitude of my sins — which in no other way could so effectively succeed for me but by this act of exposing myself, putting my life at stake in the farthest parts of the world, for Him who for my love died upon a hard wood of the Cross.",
     "Father, Your Paternity should know that this desire has so pierced my heart that of nothing else would I think, of nothing else would I speak; indeed in nothing else does it find rest but in this. And so I pray Your Paternity to look upon me with the eyes with which Christ on the Cross regarded the good thief when He said to him, hodie mecum eris in Paradiso (today thou shalt be with me in paradise) — and that Your Paternity do the same to me, calling me, so that I may be one of those holy men who are sent forth for the Kingdom of Paradise.",
     "By divine grace I am sound and whole; by this path I hope, by the Divine mercy, to make myself holy, as I indicated to Your Paternity in another letter, of which, if it please Your Paternity, you will receive information from the same Father Basile. I finish, so as not to weary you further, by humbly asking the blessing of Your Paternity, praying to heaven for every grace from the Lord for you — though unworthily — and Your Paternity should know that I am precisely a soul in Purgatory, awaiting the Angel to bear me to heaven; and for me that Angel will be Your Paternity, admitting me to such dignity as to be one of those who go to die for their God. And I shall never forget Your Paternity, so that the Lord may lavishly give you every grace from heaven."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "hist-26",
