  "destination": "china",
  "dossier": "Wrote 7 letters from Ferrara, Mantua, Modena, Parma (1617–1618) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destinations: Brazil (4), China (2), East Indies (1).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 26,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "February 13, 1617",
    "place": "Ferrara",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 118",
    "excerpt": "Most beloved Father: for the space of two years I have been held in a great desire to go toward the kingdom of China; and while the Reverend Father Giordano Cassino was provincial of the Venetian province, to me who begged it he gave authority to write to you about this matter. Behold, then, I humbly beseech you to grant me the permission to go thither, though unworthy. Nor refuse, for God has shown to me in prayer that in those parts I must die a martyr. quod Deus coniunxit homo non separet (What God hath joined together, let no man put asunder.) Do not say that I have not yet studied philosophy (for I am now giving myself to rhetoric), because He who teaches philosophy to all, teaches it also to me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Most beloved Father: for the space of two years I have been held in a great desire to go toward the kingdom of China; and while the Reverend Father Giordano Cassino was provincial of the Venetian province, to me who begged it he gave authority to write to you about this matter. Behold, then, I humbly beseech you to grant me the permission to go thither, though unworthy. Nor refuse, for God has shown to me in prayer that in those parts I must die a martyr. quod Deus coniunxit homo non separet (What God hath joined together, let no man put asunder.) Do not say that I have not yet studied philosophy (for I am now giving myself to rhetoric), because He who teaches philosophy to all, teaches it also to me. Cum tradent vos, nolite cogitare quomodo, aut quid loquamini; dabitur enim vobis in illa hora quid loquamini. Non enim estis vos, qui loquimini, sed spiritus patris vestri qui loquitur in vobis. (When they shall deliver you up, take no thought how or what to speak; for it shall be given you in that hour what to speak. For it is not you that speak, but the Spirit of your Father that speaketh in you.) Nevertheless, do now what God wills. And let this be the first, and the last, letter on this matter — lest I trouble you. Farewell."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 35,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "October 6, 1617",
    "place": "Parma",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 242",
    "excerpt": "What I once wrote to Your Paternity, I now write to Your Paternity once again by the will of the Holy Spirit, and I shall never cease until Your Paternity has granted me what I ask. Behold, then, I open to Your Paternity the secrets which God has rooted in my heart, and into your most benign hands I consign them with all my affection — not as one utters what one would rather not say, but as one speaks to a most lovable Shepherd, as Your Paternity is: something I have to scatter throughout the whole universe of the world, better than Triptolemus ever scattered the grain with his right hand.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "What I once wrote to Your Paternity, I now write to Your Paternity once again by the will of the Holy Spirit, and I shall never cease until Your Paternity has granted me what I ask. Behold, then, I open to Your Paternity the secrets which God has rooted in my heart, and into your most benign hands I consign them with all my affection — not as one utters what one would rather not say, but as one speaks to a most lovable Shepherd, as Your Paternity is: something I have to scatter throughout the whole universe of the world, better than Triptolemus ever scattered the grain with his right hand. But to open to Your Paternity what I wish, I say that I desire to go to China (as I have already asked), because I know for certain that this is the will of God, He having revealed it to me in prayer; and also because I feel, and continually experience, that I have always been inclined thither, both in the world and much more in Religion. Now it is for Your Paternity to execute what God has set in motion, so that I may render to God that hundredfold which He requires of me; but if by chance Your Paternity should deny it me, I am of the opinion that everything will turn out for me topsyturvy. Let not Your Paternity say what is going about — that I am increasing the pot before the lentils — for the spirit of God goes where it pleases; he is not a weathercock, as they say, but brings to completion the things begun and promoted by him. From this, then, let Your Paternity know my desire, which is to go find Father Nicolò Trigault before he departs further; whom having found, I shall be able with tranquil mind to attend to the study of philosophy. Let Your Paternity now take care not to contradict itself, exhorting us with such grave words to so great a work, while denying me this. Already Your Paternity has heard that messis quidem multa, operarii autem pauci (the harvest indeed is great, but the laborers are few); therefore with a generous hand let Your Paternity provide for it, and I, although unworthy, would be one of those. Therefore I pray you, by the most sacred bowels of the mercy of God, with which coming from heaven He visited us, and by His most holy passion, that as soon as possible Your Paternity send me. Be well."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 38,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "November 26, 1617",
    "place": "Parma",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 262",
    "excerpt": "I remain under the greatest obligation to Your Paternity for the remembrance lately sent me; from which I truly discern how much affection you bear your subjects. From this love springs the fact that all things are so well ordered. But by this convenient occasion, I was pleased to let Your Paternity know that it was not by the revelation that I was moved to this enterprise, but because God has so willed, giving me a prompt will; and for this I have armed my breast with a strong cuirass, to be able to bear all the adversities that will oppose the attainment of this my (or rather God’s) intention.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I remain under the greatest obligation to Your Paternity for the remembrance lately sent me; from which I truly discern how much affection you bear your subjects. From this love springs the fact that all things are so well ordered. But by this convenient occasion, I was pleased to let Your Paternity know that it was not by the revelation that I was moved to this enterprise, but because God has so willed, giving me a prompt will; and for this I have armed my breast with a strong cuirass, to be able to bear all the adversities that will oppose the attainment of this my (or rather God’s) intention. And if the young plant is still too weak to bear so great a weight, God will strengthen it, and with His grace will fence it about, so that it may not be moved from the work begun. I come, then, once more to Your Paternity, and pray you to deign to grant me this grace, so that I may labor in the new vineyard of the Lord, since He continually urges me on with fresh and vigorous goads."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 43,
    "year": 1618,
    "date": "May 18, 1618",
    "place": "Parma",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 36",
    "excerpt": "Your Paternity will forgive me if, by my frequent writing, I come to annoy you: it is God who moves me to do this; and although I am that useless servant I have always been, nonetheless, since the vocations and offices which His Divine Majesty is wont to give to men are diverse, the same, by His divine grace, gives the fitting aids and forces.\n\nWherefore, since God has called me to the salvation of souls, whether in China, or Japan, or Brazil, etc., although unworthy of such a vocation and unfit for this office, nonetheless He, who always gives grace in conformity with the office, will give me fitting forces.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Your Paternity will forgive me if, by my frequent writing, I come to annoy you: it is God who moves me to do this; and although I am that useless servant I have always been, nonetheless, since the vocations and offices which His Divine Majesty is wont to give to men are diverse, the same, by His divine grace, gives the fitting aids and forces.",
     "Wherefore, since God has called me to the salvation of souls, whether in China, or Japan, or Brazil, etc., although unworthy of such a vocation and unfit for this office, nonetheless He, who always gives grace in conformity with the office, will give me fitting forces. Therefore may Your Paternity, placed by God in such rank that you may govern His Religion, for love of Him, and for His greater honor, and the salvation of my soul, grant me leave for the next convenient occasion of the Father Procurator of Brazil (who, as we have been told, is in Rome), that I may go with him.",
     "I know full well my few and slight forces; but because God, with particular movement, has moved me to ask again for this of Your Paternity, I have written. I would not wish to have written for ceremony, nor indeed do I truly write, but that God may be the more glorified, our Society served, and the souls to whose salvation I shall be employed, and also my own, may remain saved, obtaining from Your Paternity what I ask.",
     "I shall not offer to Your Paternity, for this voyage, any instruments — that is, money and other useful things like books, etc. — being able, after I have had the license, to find them more freely, if it shall so please Your Paternity. But do not abandon me: give me what I ask; for if it be needful to ask it a thousand times and more, I shall always do so, that God may thus be glorified — nothing else. Not to weary you further: Of Your Paternity —"
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 45,
    "year": 1618,
    "date": "June 27, 1618",
    "place": "Mantua",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 53",
    "excerpt": "I write anew to Your Paternity to show him my desire. Your Paternity will forgive me if I annoy him with my letters; but because I am always moved by the desire to aid souls — that are throughout the whole world, but, by particular grace of God, those that are either in the Indies, in China, or in Brazil and other like places — therefore anew I ask him, and pray him, by that Divine Majesty, that, by the good occasion of the Father who goes to Brazil, he may be content to give me leave, that I may be able to go with him.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I write anew to Your Paternity to show him my desire. Your Paternity will forgive me if I annoy him with my letters; but because I am always moved by the desire to aid souls — that are throughout the whole world, but, by particular grace of God, those that are either in the Indies, in China, or in Brazil and other like places — therefore anew I ask him, and pray him, by that Divine Majesty, that, by the good occasion of the Father who goes to Brazil, he may be content to give me leave, that I may be able to go with him.",
     "In the last letter I wrote to Your Paternity, I promised him good things for this voyage; now anew I promise them to him, which I shall find, once he has given me the license. This is what I have to write, and I shall not lengthen my letter further, lest I annoy Your Paternity."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 48,
    "year": 1618,
    "date": "August 23, 1618",
    "place": "Mantua",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 70",
    "excerpt": "I write to Your Paternity not to disturb you with useless letters, but because a convenient occasion has offered itself, of one of ours who comes from Your Paternity, and also because His Divine Majesty, with ever greater impulses from day to day, shakes me ever more, that I ask this anew of Your Paternity. Your Paternity sees this to be the will of our Lord God; and the Father Provincial also desired to see this, who discerned that it was truly the will of God.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I write to Your Paternity not to disturb you with useless letters, but because a convenient occasion has offered itself, of one of ours who comes from Your Paternity, and also because His Divine Majesty, with ever greater impulses from day to day, shakes me ever more, that I ask this anew of Your Paternity. Your Paternity sees this to be the will of our Lord God; and the Father Provincial also desired to see this, who discerned that it was truly the will of God.",
     "I await also that Your Paternity may wish, and may explain to me his will — for my desire is so great to go either to China, or Japan, or the Indies, or Brazil, etc., that there is no hour, though I am most occupied, in which I do not remember it and say: “When will the Reverend Father General give me the license!”",
     "Your Paternity, I pray, by the passion of Jesus Christ, give this comfort to an unworthy brother of yours, who, moved by God, seeks to put into execution that which Your Paternity recommends to us in that letter of yours; and for this, trusting first in God, then in that great desire which Your Paternity shows in that letter, that we go to fish for souls in those countries, I await the license (there being so convenient an occasion at present) to be able to go with that Father to Brazil, that there I may put into execution what His Divine Majesty urges me to with frequent promptings; and in this way draw into the net of God, with the weak instrument of myself, many souls which God has reserved for me.",
     "Therefore let not Your Paternity leave me so afflicted, but consoled with giving it to me soon — for that end, as I have said — and also that I may prepare those books, etc., concerning which I would like to know whether Your Paternity is content that I begin to find them, that I may arrange to be more prepared. In this too let not Your Paternity abandon me, but see to it that in this that province may remain accommodated and satisfied. I shall not torment you more, but [I shall close] by asking anew that Your Paternity give me such license. Be well."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 49,
    "year": 1618,
    "date": "October 30, 1618",
    "place": "Modena",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 88",
    "excerpt": "Your Paternity will perhaps accuse me because I am too importunate in writing to you; but know that until you have fulfilled my desire to go to the Indies, etc., you will always mark me for this. To tell the truth, I can find rest neither day nor night, for I always have my thought fixed, meditating in this fashion: when will Your Paternity give me that so-desired license to go to the Indies, that there I may employ my forces and my life in the salvation of those souls; and in this fashion awaiting the desired rest, which will bring me rest while I labor much.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Your Paternity will perhaps accuse me because I am too importunate in writing to you; but know that until you have fulfilled my desire to go to the Indies, etc., you will always mark me for this. To tell the truth, I can find rest neither day nor night, for I always have my thought fixed, meditating in this fashion: when will Your Paternity give me that so-desired license to go to the Indies, that there I may employ my forces and my life in the salvation of those souls; and in this fashion awaiting the desired rest, which will bring me rest while I labor much. And thinking that it had arrived, when there arrived at Modena those two Fathers, one of whom was going to Portugal, the other to Japan, whom I should accompany, I stood all glad at seeing them; but then, on hearing there was no news of me, although I conformed myself to the will of God — in all things a consoler of adversity — nonetheless I could not help being somewhat saddened.",
     "In Your Paternity I trust that as soon as possible you will show me the course in which, running and laboring for our Lord God, I shall find rest; and I pray you, for the love of the Most Blessed Virgin Mary, if anything avails with Your Paternity, and for the love of Christ, who stimulates me to so great a work — for so it pleases Him — give me as soon as possible license to depart to some part of the world, to the gaining of souls for Heaven, whether in Japan, China, Brazil, etc. And I, meanwhile, while I wait, shall attend to my school, which the Father Provincial has given me in Modena."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
