  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 27,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "March 10, 1617",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 122",
    "excerpt": "Seeing the time approach when there is hope that missions to the Indies may be made, I am compelled by the desire to be one of those who shall be sent to those happy parts — I am compelled, I say, to refresh Your Paternity's memory, that I wait with the utmost longing for your call, hoping most firmly in the Lord, and in the charity which His Divine Majesty has placed in your heart toward us, His sons, that you will console me as soon as possible. I do not know what more to do to obtain this grace; I know nothing else to do but to pray His Divine Majesty to make me worthy of it — and since He has given me the desire, to bring it about that I fulfill what He wants of me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Seeing the time approach when there is hope that missions to the Indies may be made, I am compelled by the desire to be one of those who shall be sent to those happy parts — I am compelled, I say, to refresh Your Paternity's memory, that I wait with the utmost longing for your call, hoping most firmly in the Lord, and in the charity which His Divine Majesty has placed in your heart toward us, His sons, that you will console me as soon as possible. I do not know what more to do to obtain this grace; I know nothing else to do but to pray His Divine Majesty to make me worthy of it — and since He has given me the desire, to bring it about that I fulfill what He wants of me. It is true He must do all, yet because He wills that we apply all diligence, I have recourse to you, Holy Father, as His minister, who represents His person to me — praying you to grant me what the Lord asks of me. I do nothing else but make an end, commending myself to your Holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 32,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "May 5, 1617",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 150",
    "excerpt": "Every greeting from these Fathers, given me on behalf of Your Paternity, kindles and inflames my heart the more to ask Your Paternity with greater insistence for the Indies I so desire; and at the same time I take no little consolation in seeing the remembrance Your Paternity keeps of consoling me, directing me as soon as possible toward those lands — for which I cannot but thank you with all my heart — and I ask of God Our Lord that He inspire you to grant me what He demands of me, as of the one who stands in His place and is the interpreter of His divine will.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Every greeting from these Fathers, given me on behalf of Your Paternity, kindles and inflames my heart the more to ask Your Paternity with greater insistence for the Indies I so desire; and at the same time I take no little consolation in seeing the remembrance Your Paternity keeps of consoling me, directing me as soon as possible toward those lands — for which I cannot but thank you with all my heart — and I ask of God Our Lord that He inspire you to grant me what He demands of me, as of the one who stands in His place and is the interpreter of His divine will. Then I turn to Your Paternity, setting before you my desire, and from you I await its immediate fulfillment. And I beg you most earnestly to condescend to what I have asked you, so that I may employ myself wholly and entirely, and in a certain particular way, for God Our Lord, suffering for Him and helping those souls for whom He chose to give His own life and shed all His blood. For the rest, I commend myself to your holy prayers and sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 41,
    "year": 1618,
    "date": "February 9, 1618",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 12",
    "excerpt": "Many are the reasons and motives that impel me to write and to remind Your Paternity of my desire for the Indies: for if I turn toward the East, I see the blood shed by the holy Christians, which, like seed, promises most beautiful shoots and great fruit of souls, and consequently demands cultivators and laborers of that blessed land, that the shoot, being cultivated, may be preserved, and come at last to perfection, and bear that fruit which our Lord God expects of us; and if this should not come to pass, I stand in great fear lest He command that we not set sail, and that those souls, left to themselves, hurl themselves headlong into the infernal abyss.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Many are the reasons and motives that impel me to write and to remind Your Paternity of my desire for the Indies: for if I turn toward the East, I see the blood shed by the holy Christians, which, like seed, promises most beautiful shoots and great fruit of souls, and consequently demands cultivators and laborers of that blessed land, that the shoot, being cultivated, may be preserved, and come at last to perfection, and bear that fruit which our Lord God expects of us; and if this should not come to pass, I stand in great fear lest He command that we not set sail, and that those souls, left to themselves, hurl themselves headlong into the infernal abyss. And this fruit has at times not been borne for lack of care and cultivation — wherefore it is mine to offer myself to Your Paternity and to pray you instantly that you may deign to grant me to serve the Lord; the voice calls me.",
     "On the other hand, I feel myself drawn by the example of our brothers, who with such charity and love toward our Lord God, and from their great desire to convert souls, did not hesitate to expose themselves to death for the holy faith, and indeed left their lives there. Would to God that I might one day be their humble imitator; and if I should not be worthy of this, at least let not Your Paternity deny my going to those parts where this desire may easily be put into execution — for I shall live content with the mere hope that the same might befall me, if my defects do not impede it. I close, commending myself to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 44,
    "year": 1618,
    "date": "May 25, 1618",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 44",
    "excerpt": "As the Lord urges me with continual desires that I ask of Your Paternity to go to the Indies, I am compelled to put it into execution by means of letters, since in no other way can I do it. And I do it now the more willingly, inasmuch as it seems to me the time is near when the Lord will grant me this grace, by His infinite goodness and mercy; for since He has given me the desire, He will also give me the accomplishing of it; and what gladdens me most is that He will not delay to console me — which in the second place I expect from Your Paternity, to whom it has been committed by God to deny or to grant me this grace.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "As the Lord urges me with continual desires that I ask of Your Paternity to go to the Indies, I am compelled to put it into execution by means of letters, since in no other way can I do it. And I do it now the more willingly, inasmuch as it seems to me the time is near when the Lord will grant me this grace, by His infinite goodness and mercy; for since He has given me the desire, He will also give me the accomplishing of it; and what gladdens me most is that He will not delay to console me — which in the second place I expect from Your Paternity, to whom it has been committed by God to deny or to grant me this grace.",
     "I ask nothing else of him but to be able to go and to suffer something for the love of Christ Jesus, and to employ myself wholly in the aid of souls redeemed with the price of His divine Blood — this being the institute to which the Lord has called me; and although this exercise is found also in these parts, yet in my ears resound the tearful voices of those poor gentiles who ask for salvation, and perhaps there lacks one to bring it to them. And it moves me all the more that, without thought of this, they continually descend to hell without other remedy.",
     "I pray therefore Your Paternity that, among the others you will send for the aid of those peoples, you may number me, though unworthy and unfit; for I hope in the Lord that He will give me all that is needful for such a mission. For the rest, I commend myself to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 50,
    "year": 1618,
    "date": "December 1618",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 98",
    "excerpt": "Although the seeing of our companions called to go and cultivate the field of the Lord in the Indies, and our being left behind, bring us a good and holy envy, and an inconsolable desire to be their companions — nevertheless this same gives us hope that one day the same may befall us, with as much gladness as we discern in them; and the pain of our delayed calling is dulled by thinking of the boundless content the Lord will grant us, when we shall set out to those parts, where now we see our companions make the way for us by preceding us.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Although the seeing of our companions called to go and cultivate the field of the Lord in the Indies, and our being left behind, bring us a good and holy envy, and an inconsolable desire to be their companions — nevertheless this same gives us hope that one day the same may befall us, with as much gladness as we discern in them; and the pain of our delayed calling is dulled by thinking of the boundless content the Lord will grant us, when we shall set out to those parts, where now we see our companions make the way for us by preceding us. May it please the Lord that such a happy day be near!",
     "And may Your Paternity — by means of whom, in doing this, the Lord God makes use — for the bowels of the charity of Christ, shorten the days and call us soon, not looking at the unfitness of such a subject, but trusting from the Lord every help and grace for that to which He calls us. Nothing more. For the rest, I commend myself to your holy prayers and sacrifices — I commend myself greatly."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "hist-29",
  "fictional": false,
  "name": "Diego Camassa",
  "author_raw": "Camassa, Diego",
  "age": null,
  "yearsInSociety": null,
  "formation": null,
  "studies": null,
  "skills": [],
  "health": null,
  "assignment": null,
  "destination": "east-indies",
  "dossier": "Wrote 6 letters from Naples (1617–1618) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destinations: Japan (4), East Indies (2).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 29,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "April 21, 1617",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 136",
    "excerpt": "At last I am no longer a child; my strength is whole, and my health excellent. I believe this is the time I have so long awaited, to go where God calls me — to the salvation of so many souls that are perishing; to gather that precious blood of Christ which is spilled upon the earth; to preach with my blood — O happy Diego, if that shall be! And I await it, my Father; I know well that, having ever been assured in this my vocation by so many most just ways through my superiors and spiritual fathers: I was born for the Indies — nor shall anyone take them from me; they are mine, and I must give a strict account of them before the tribunal of the great God.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "At last I am no longer a child; my strength is whole, and my health excellent. I believe this is the time I have so long awaited, to go where God calls me — to the salvation of so many souls that are perishing; to gather that precious blood of Christ which is spilled upon the earth; to preach with my blood — O happy Diego, if that shall be! And I await it, my Father; I know well that, having ever been assured in this my vocation by so many most just ways through my superiors and spiritual fathers: I was born for the Indies — nor shall anyone take them from me; they are mine, and I must give a strict account of them before the tribunal of the great God. Thus has that infinite mercy and power been pleased: infimum mundi eligere (to choose the lowest thing of the world) for so great a ministry. Let Him do as He will. I know well that since Your Paternity is continually that which the Lord has [...], it will not fail me. Yet, not to fail myself — rather, to fail God — I know what is mine to do. And I [put myself] in the hands and at the feet of Your Paternity, with total indifference in all that you shall dispose of me. Then I remind you of what I proposed to you at other times: that I was born and entered this most happy and most mighty Society of Jesus to subdue to God not one city, or one kingdom, but a hundred thousand worlds, were there so many. I feel, Father, the blaze grown each day ever greater in my heart, and it inflames me to this noble conquest. Let Your Paternity now at last let me follow my Captain to the Indies, for it is time. He has opened to me an immense vastness; and O, what fair fruits await! The many difficulties which I know I shall have to bear do not frighten me, for I have one who will give me heart and strength. Rest assured, my Father, that there either the glorious death for Christ (O would it were so!) or something shall be. It suffices to make well known the God who has chosen me, a poor man, ne glorietur in conspectu eius omnis caro (that no flesh should glory in His sight). Most mercifully I only affirm to Your Paternity that it stands in your hand that I become an apostle — on God's part there is no lack; and I have already offered, and do offer, myself, most feeble instrument; I have no doubt at all — but in the hand of that Son qui vocat ea, quae non sunt, tanquam ea quae sunt (who calleth those things that are not, as though they were), [...] what He wills. I finish. My Father, it is time; there is no need (I must study) to finish my studies here, for as many reasons as Your Paternity knows. And if indeed you wished me first to finish philosophy — which I wish were not so — at least, O my Father, [make] me sure. Open my heart, O most sweet and only Father, who are to me in the place of Jesus Christ. I burn, my Father, I burn, and I write it with tears in my eyes. Behold my heart in the hands and at the feet of Your Paternity, with all indifference; nor shall I depart from you ever, until I am fully, fully consoled. Do me this grace, my Father; for, though I am unworthy of it, I am your son. And I do not seek it for myself, but for Jesus Christ crucified and for the Holy Church of God. Grant it to me for love of the Most Blessed Virgin, for our Holy Father Ignatius, and for all the court of Heaven. I follow Your Paternity in the Holy Sacrifices and prayers daily; and I pray the Lord for every good for you, and the will to console me."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 30,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "April 21, 1617",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 139",
    "excerpt": "I ought not trouble Your Paternity further with new letters, since the last is still alive. But that thing which I put in it — namely that Your Paternity, if you would not send me now to the Indies, would at least assure me for after philosophy — was most foolish, and ungrateful to God. This is not what the fire I feel required, nor the confidence I must have. Had I need to be more assured in a matter so clear — which is [almost] a revelation, if not to me, at least to all my superiors and spiritual fathers?",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I ought not trouble Your Paternity further with new letters, since the last is still alive. But that thing which I put in it — namely that Your Paternity, if you would not send me now to the Indies, would at least assure me for after philosophy — was most foolish, and ungrateful to God. This is not what the fire I feel required, nor the confidence I must have. Had I need to be more assured in a matter so clear — which is [almost] a revelation, if not to me, at least to all my superiors and spiritual fathers? Enough. I repent. My Father, in the bowels of Jesus Christ: I see (let it be said, as it truly is, with all indifference) — I see, my Father, that now God wants me in the Indies, or in Japan, in so many occasions which Your Paternity knows well. I cannot wait for a better time. Now nothing is lacking to me; indeed, as it has pleased the infinite mercy of God [in everything I have]. My Father, I write — I know not how — with tears. Enough of what I have written to you. I present to you that most precious blood of Christ crucified; the salvation of infinite souls; and of my own soul. I shall be an apostle, for so God wills. The cause is not mine. I put all in the hands of Your Paternity, and myself at your feet. I await the most happy news. And meanwhile I do not cease to pray, as I have ever done, for Your Paternity. Help me, my Father, for you are my Father to me."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 33,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "May 12, 1617",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 158",
    "excerpt": "At last I have received the most happy news for me, and so long awaited — may God be forever blessed. Now indeed all my desires are fulfilled, and, what matters most, by the one who is for me the interpreter of the divine will. O happy me! My Father! I have been left thunderstruck, and as if beside myself, by the infinite mercy of God — may He be blessed again and infinitely; I cannot contain myself. Forgive me, Your Paternity, for I cannot and do not know how to express what I feel. I know nothing else, except that I have placed myself in the hands of the good and great God, and I believe He has accepted me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "At last I have received the most happy news for me, and so long awaited — may God be forever blessed. Now indeed all my desires are fulfilled, and, what matters most, by the one who is for me the interpreter of the divine will. O happy me! My Father! I have been left thunderstruck, and as if beside myself, by the infinite mercy of God — may He be blessed again and infinitely; I cannot contain myself. Forgive me, Your Paternity, for I cannot and do not know how to express what I feel. I know nothing else, except that I have placed myself in the hands of the good and great God, and I believe He has accepted me. He will know well how to make use of me. Let hell rage; I am in good hands, and I no longer fear contradictions. Only it remains (of which I have no doubt) that Your Paternity keep me continually at your feet; and with the holy Sacrifices and prayers obtain for me from the Lord, as the final completion, that heart and spirit which are necessary for such an enterprise. With which I make you most humble reverence, giving you infinite thanks."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 34,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "June 16, 1617",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 186",
    "excerpt": "May that infinite Goodness of God be forever blessed, who is never sated with doing us good. I wrote to Your Paternity that I had placed myself in the hands of God, already sure of His divine will, which calls me to Japan by so many signs, and because Your Paternity, who stands in His place for me, has so interpreted it for me. Let all of hell now take arms — O, in what good hands do I find myself! He will know well how to keep me and make use of me in His time. Nor do I believe that my sins can stand against that incomprehensible Mercy, nor against the prayers of Your Paternity joined with those of the most precious Mother, of our Reverend Fathers Ignatius and Xavier, and of so many saints, who I believe stand ever helping me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "May that infinite Goodness of God be forever blessed, who is never sated with doing us good. I wrote to Your Paternity that I had placed myself in the hands of God, already sure of His divine will, which calls me to Japan by so many signs, and because Your Paternity, who stands in His place for me, has so interpreted it for me. Let all of hell now take arms — O, in what good hands do I find myself! He will know well how to keep me and make use of me in His time. Nor do I believe that my sins can stand against that incomprehensible Mercy, nor against the prayers of Your Paternity joined with those of the most precious Mother, of our Reverend Fathers Ignatius and Xavier, and of so many saints, who I believe stand ever helping me. I did not trouble to ask Your Paternity to call me to Rome, because I do not think I stand less at your feet — with the confidence I owe to so many Fathers — than if I were in Rome bodily present. Yet Your Paternity well knows that the devil on such occasions does not fail to make his effort to disturb everything; and God wills that we too labor with His grace in what we can. I write therefore to Your Paternity lest I have to hear of some disturbance; for affection makes me tremble where there is no cause to doubt. I have breathed no word to my relatives. Now I understand that they know it — I know not from whom among Ours (and this because the Father Rector published it, whence many knew it before me). My father has written me nothing of it. I do not know whether some plot is intended. My Father: I have no other Father but God and Your Paternity, to whom I have given myself wholly; guard me then, for love of that most precious blood of Christ, to the end with your prudence — not because I doubt, but because prayers are necessary when the devil puts himself in the middle. Carlo Camassa, who was my father, I have already left, with the whole world. Nothing more is needed there. He has no need of me at all. He has a thousand helps where there is no need. He has the means to live. He has well provided for the daughters; there remains to him in the world a son already grown and married; another in the Society with Father Giovanni Filippo Camassa, who is his brother, to close his eyes — and I am his no longer. I am God’s, of that most holy blood of Jesus Christ, and of so many poor little souls, delivered to Your Paternity as most unworthy son, in whose hands and at whose feet (so I feel impelled) I renew and make the vow of Obedience — particularly to go, where I am sent, to Japan (if it shall please you to accept it) — of Poverty and perpetual Chastity; and to enter this most holy Society of Jesus to live and die in it, offering myself wholly as a holocaust to the grace of God, although most unworthy, yet confiding in the most precious blood of Jesus Christ. And so I finish, no longer with fear, but cheerful and secure. I remain at the feet of Your Paternity, begging you in conclusion to obtain for me from the Lord heart and spirit to correspond to so great a Goodness of God. And forgive me the length."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 37,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "October 24, 1617",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 249",
    "excerpt": "I have learned from the letter of the Father Assistant of Portugal that Your Paternity, for I know not what news from Japan, wishes to defer our departure to next year, when we should have arrived to do some good. God knows how I have felt it on the one hand. It was not enough for the devil that he would have driven me from Japan, unless he also confines us in Italy, shut up as in a narrow prison. And what good is it that the Lord has made me overcome so many difficulties, since when I thought to enjoy the grace so long awaited, and now received, I see myself so far from it!",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I have learned from the letter of the Father Assistant of Portugal that Your Paternity, for I know not what news from Japan, wishes to defer our departure to next year, when we should have arrived to do some good. God knows how I have felt it on the one hand. It was not enough for the devil that he would have driven me from Japan, unless he also confines us in Italy, shut up as in a narrow prison. And what good is it that the Lord has made me overcome so many difficulties, since when I thought to enjoy the grace so long awaited, and now received, I see myself so far from it! My father sought nothing else, that meanwhile some other impediment might arise. But I hope, by the infinite goodness of God, it will not be so, everything being ordered by the most sweet hand of His divine providence through Your Paternity. This alone certainly makes me, instead of sighing continually, remain secure and cheerful; and I console myself that we are not the masters of God’s glory. Enough. That most precious blood of the Crucified will bring everything to its end, for He began it, since the cause is His, not ours. So I thank Your Paternity, who has given me the opportunity to make a sacrifice to His Divine Majesty; and certainly it is no small sacrifice for me. But I pray Your Paternity that, if the difficulties are surmountable, you not leave us, for the love of God, any longer disconsolate in this matter; for we are still in time. I would ask that Your Paternity call us meanwhile to Rome, so that I may stand more secure at your feet (for it seems to me that business is handled differently in person than by letters; and indeed those who were in Rome have departed — blessed be God). But nescio quid petam (I know not what to ask) — I know not what I ask. Your Paternity wishes me more good than I can imagine. If you assure me that staying in Naples does not make me more distant from Your Paternity than I would be in Rome, this too can be offered to the Lord. My Father, I stand clean in your hands: make and unmake of me as you please, provided that with your holy Sacrifices and prayers you keep me within the wounds of the Crucified, so that before going to Japan I may learn to navigate that immense ocean of the charity of God."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 42,
    "year": 1618,
    "date": "April 6, 1618",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 24",
    "excerpt": "I write this to Your Paternity, so as not to lose the good occasion of these holy days to give a thousand good holiday greetings to Your Paternity — indeed to myself — and I have awaited it these many months for no small interest of my own. Believe me, my Father, that since October, since I have not written to Your Paternity, the hours seem years to me until I arrive at my Japan; nor have I had any other consolation, truly, in this time, than the sure hope in the determination of the Lord, upon the word of Your Paternity, who with your charity has opened to me your heart forever.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I write this to Your Paternity, so as not to lose the good occasion of these holy days to give a thousand good holiday greetings to Your Paternity — indeed to myself — and I have awaited it these many months for no small interest of my own. Believe me, my Father, that since October, since I have not written to Your Paternity, the hours seem years to me until I arrive at my Japan; nor have I had any other consolation, truly, in this time, than the sure hope in the determination of the Lord, upon the word of Your Paternity, who with your charity has opened to me your heart forever. You have promised me a good that is like a non plus ultra (no further beyond) of what I could desire in this life.",
     "I am at peace, my Father, and I know that Your Paternity, imitating the loving bowels of blessed Christ Crucified, who knows how to do nothing but good, even to one who deserves nothing but every evil — I know that you love me, oh, how much more than I merit. May the Lord and the Most Holy Mother render to you for me, in that eminent manner of yours, what I cannot.",
     "But if anything can be added to the charity Your Paternity shows me, give me leave to ask of you one thing for its completion: that Your Paternity, not forgetting our exile, for the love of God call us quickly to Rome at the beginning of September, for it will be a most particular consolation both for Brother Girolamo and for me, to find ourselves soon in Rome, to have time to visit those holy places, and, if it shall so please, to wander as pilgrims to the Holy House, besides placing ourselves in greater safety — for one who loves, my Father, cannot see the hour, and must of necessity fear.",
     "With this promise, I would desire Your Paternity to give me the Easter good wishes. And I meanwhile do not cease to pray in my cold prayers for the health of Your Paternity. May the Lord grant to Your Paternity every day a greater abundance of His treasures, for the universal good, and for those poor Japanese souls, and for the Society, and for the whole Church. And, making you most humble reverence, I commend myself to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
